    "prerequisite_note": "Review QA Principles: evidence can show a discrepancy without establishing universal correctness or cause.",
    "practice": "What belongs in the actual-result field?|Observed behavior|Only a proposed cause|Only a planned fix|Only the desired result|The actual result records the observation that differs from the expected rule.|A cause needs separate evidence;A planned change is not an observation;The desired result belongs in expectation.\nWhat makes an expected result defensible?|A stated oracle|Reporter confidence alone|A guessed round number|A copied actual value|A rule or independent example supplies a basis for judging the observed behavior.|Confidence is not a rule;An arbitrary value lacks justification;Copying actual output cannot challenge it.\nA fee error is titled database corruption without storage evidence. What should change?|Separate hypothesis|Increase its certainty|Hide the actual total|Remove all context|The report should distinguish observed mismatch from an unproven explanation.|More certainty does not add evidence;The mismatch is useful evidence;Context is needed for interpretation.\nA wrong total occurs with a concession enabled. Which setup matters?|Concession state|Only the reporter name|Only the issue number|Only the meeting date|The relevant business state affects the expected fee calculation.|Reporter identity does not specify policy;An issue identifier is not a precondition;A meeting date does not define the account state.\nAn investigator discovers new evidence. What preserves history?|Append scoped findings|Rewrite the first observation|Delete the old artifact ID|Claim the cause was known|New findings should retain the original observation and identify their own scope.|Rewriting obscures what happened;Artifact identity supports applicability;Later knowledge should not be backdated.\nA report requests a specific implementation before the rule is agreed. What is the risk?|Premature solution bias|Improved reproduction|A stronger oracle by default|Guaranteed faster resolution|A proposed implementation may anchor investigation before the intended behavior is resolved.|A solution is not reproduction data;An implementation proposal does not validate the rule;Anchoring can waste effort.",
    "formal": "Why distinguish expected and actual results?|Expose the discrepancy|Avoid all requirements|Guarantee a known cause|Replace reproduction|A clear contrast identifies what failed under the reported conditions.|An oracle still needs a rule;The contrast does not prove cause;Reproduction adds essential context.\nWhich title is behavior-specific?|Fee omits concession|System completely broken|Developer made an error|Database definitely corrupt|A focused title describes the symptom without unsupported attribution.|A broad label hides scope;Blame does not explain behavior;Corruption requires evidence.\nWhat does a root-cause hypothesis represent?|A proposed explanation|An established observation|A release authorization|A verified correction|A hypothesis suggests a mechanism that still needs discriminating evidence.|An observation is directly recorded;A cause theory does not authorize release;A proposed cause is not a tested fix.\nAn LMS total is wrong only for one discount category. What should its report include?|Relevant category and inputs|Every real student record|Only a screenshot of the homepage|Only the proposed patch|The category and inputs define the conditions needed to understand the discrepancy.|Unrelated private records add exposure;The homepage omits the calculation;A patch alone does not show the failing case.\nA retailer\u2019s report says sometimes wrong with no expected example. What is missing?|A checkable discrepancy|A higher priority label|An assigned developer|A larger attachment|The reader needs an actual observation and defensible expected outcome before diagnosis.|Priority does not define behavior;Assignment cannot invent an oracle;More bytes do not clarify the mismatch.\nA bank\u2019s rounding report uses the same function for expected and actual output. What should be reviewed?|Oracle independence|Only its severity color|Only test execution speed|Only issue chronology|The same faulty computation can reproduce an error on both sides of the comparison.|Severity does not repair the oracle;Speed is unrelated to independence;Ordering does not justify the expected value.\nA hospital report claims data loss after a display is empty. What is appropriate?|Record symptom and test causes|Declare storage loss proven|Delete the empty view evidence|Assume no impact exists|An empty view can reflect filtering, access or actual storage loss, so the cause needs evidence.|The display alone does not prove persistence loss;The observed view is useful;Lack of proof of loss is not proof of safety.\nA logistics report includes a suspected cache cause. How should it be presented?|Clearly as a hypothesis|As the immutable title truth|Instead of reproduction steps|As proof all servers fail|A scoped hypothesis can help investigation while preserving uncertainty.|A title should not freeze an unproven cause;Steps remain useful;One suspicion does not establish population scope.\nA SaaS report is updated after a rule clarification. What should remain visible?|Original and revised oracle scope|Only the newest narrative|A fictional initial agreement|Only the closed status|The report should explain how the expected behavior changed without rewriting earlier facts.|Replacing history conceals the change;Agreement cannot be backdated;A status omits the reasoning.\nA university disagreement is about whether the rule or implementation is wrong. What advances triage?|Evidence and rule owner|Immediate blame assignment|A random expected value|Deleting the issue|A shared example and authoritative policy help separate requirement ambiguity from implementation mismatch.|Blame cannot resolve the rule;Random values are not an oracle;Deletion discards useful evidence."
